Differential privacy has emerged as a standard framework for ensuring privacy in statistical analysis and machine learning across numerous domains. Since its introduction, multiple differential privacy mechanisms have been developed for data with relational structures, including matrix mechanisms, subsampling mechanisms, and differential privacy for distribution estimation. Among these, the differential privacy mechanism for distribution estimation is the most fundamental. It was initially proposed by Dwork and Rothblum in 2009 and later termed the $\epsilon$-differentially private mechanism for distribution estimation by Kasiviswanathan and Lei in 2012. This core mechanism underpins several advanced mechanisms, most notably the matrix mechanism and the bounded-differences mechanism.

